{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-96063","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3072"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1442714732#0_2935155987","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3072","rank":9,"snippet":"useful while trying to find a new address, or when going on a road trip for older adults. Sure, but little kids don’t drive, right? So why should they learn to use maps, let alone make them? We will be exploring the benefits of young children learning how to make maps, and we’ll throw in some ideas of how to make that happen. Why maps are an important tool for a child’s learning development 1. Learning about maps introduces spatial thinking<cover>Learning about maps helps children begin to understand what is going on in the spaces around them. According to National Geographic, “Spatial thinking is one of the most important skills that students </cover>can develop as they learn geography, Earth, and environmental sciences.” 2. Reading maps improves visual literacy According to learnnc.org, map skills contribute to the skill of reading images. This means that a student is comprehending an image, rather than just observing it.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Teaching young children about maps for learning development\" , \"url\": \"https://www.schooliseasy.com/2016/02/teaching-young-children-"},"time":9},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":12,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
